## Identifying Executables with netstat -b

Another useful option is:

netstat -b

The `-b` option displays the executable associated with network connections and listening ports where Windows can determine it.

This provides another way to investigate:

Which program is responsible for this network activity?

The executable may appear in brackets alongside the connection information.

For example:

[application.exe]

TCP   <local-address>   <remote-address>:443   ESTABLISHED

`netstat -b` normally requires an elevated Command Prompt and may take longer to produce its output.

This is related to the investigation I performed using:

netstat -ano

followed by:

tasklist /FI "PID eq <PID>"

The two approaches provide different ways of identifying the process or executable associated with network activity.

---

## Comparing Useful Netstat Commands

netstat

Shows active network connections.

netstat -ano

Shows active connections, listening ports, numerical addresses and PIDs.

netstat -ano | findstr ESTABLISHED

Filters the results to active established TCP connections.

netstat -r

Displays the IP routing table.

netstat -b

Displays the executable associated with network connections where available.

tasklist /FI "PID eq <PID>"

Identifies the process associated with a PID obtained from `netstat -ano`.

