## What I Learned

I learned how to use `netstat` to investigate network activity on a Windows system.

I can identify active connections, listening ports, loopback traffic and TCP connection states.

I can use `netstat -ano` to identify the PID responsible for a connection and use `tasklist` to identify the associated process.

I also learned that `netstat -b` can help identify executables associated with network connections, while `netstat -r` displays the routing table used by Windows to determine where network traffic should be sent.

Together, these commands give me several ways to investigate connectivity, routing, ports and application network activity during Windows troubleshooting.
